      "html": "<ul><li>Origin: Brazil</li><li>Available size: 10-15 cm</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/pterophyllum_scalare_santa_isabel_en/",
      "html": "<p>Wild-collected angelfish from the municipality of Santa Isabel on the Rio Negro, Brazil.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/pterophyllum_scalare_santa_isabel_en/",
      "html": "<p>The water in the upper reaches of the Rio Negro is very soft and acidic.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/pterophyllum_scalare_santa_isabel_en/",
      "html": "<p>Like many other fishes from that area, mainly discus and Apistogramma, these angelfish show a high degree of red coloration on the body. The reason for this phenomenon is unknown.</p><p>Like all larger wild fish, these angels have had experience with fin-eating piranhas. However, they are very healthy and stable and regenerate their finnage very fast, so after a few weeks nothing of the injuries from the wild will be visible any more.</p>"
    }
  ],
